        "original_id": "7d45a1a2-a881-5b4f-beea-25251ac59157",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Rensselaer Plateau, Adirondack Mountains, Round Pond, Berlin, New York, eastern Quebec, Nova Scotia, New England, Appalachians, western North Carolina\n- Plants: Picea rubens, red spruce, Eastern White Pine, Eastern Hemlock, Balsam Fir, Red Pine, Tamarack\n- Concepts: vegetation, geologic formation, native range\n\nFacts:\n- The Rensselaer Plateau has vegetation more similar to the Adirondack Mountains than surrounding areas.\n- The Rensselaer Plateau has abundant Eastern White Pine, Eastern Hemlock, Red Spruce, and Balsam Fir.\n- The Rensselaer Plateau has more limited occurrences of Red Pine and Tamarack.\n- Picea rubens, commonly known as red spruce, is a spruce species native to eastern North America.\n- Red spruce ranges from eastern Quebec to Nova Scotia and from New England south through the Adirondack Mountains and Appalachians to western North Carolina.\n- Round Pond in Berlin, New York, is located on the geologic formation known as the Rensselaer Plateau.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "7c1f8042-85bd-5aab-bf56-570c875b1f80",
        "text": "Page: Rensselaer Plateau. Sentence: Vegetation on the plateau is more similar to that found in the Adirondack Mountains to the northwest, with abundant Eastern White Pine, Eastern Hemlock, Red Spruce, and Balsam Fir, along with more limited occurrences of Red Pine and Tamarack.\nPage: Picea rubens. Sentence: Picea rubens, commonly known as red spruce, is a species of spruce native to eastern North America, ranging from eastern Quebec to Nova Scotia, and from New England south in the Adirondack Mountains and Appalachians to western North Carolina.\nPage: Round Pond (Berlin, New York). Sentence: The lake is located on a geologic formation known as the Rensselaer Plateau.",
